!! viz-alert.allspeak — the editor's drawing notice, as a companion module.
!!
!! The panel that says a drawing is under way, what it expects to cost and what it did cost: the box near the top of the page that the editor also uses for its own messages (`se-alert`, and the text inside it). It is a module for the same reason the pane and the sidebar are — the editor needs none of it until somebody opens a run — and it is a *separate* module from both for the reason those two are separate from each other: what it is about is neither the picture nor the reading, and the page gives it the same box the editor already renders.
!!
!! What it owns, and what it does not. It owns the panel and the whole of its life — when it goes up, what it says, and when it goes back down — and nothing else. **The prediction is not here.** It is the pane's, because the pane measures the drawing and shows the estimate in the picture's own corner, so it needs the number where the text that shows it is; a round trip through a message queue would put the estimate on the far side of a queue from the sentence that has to display it. The sentences arrive assembled. **This is a panel that says what it is told**: it holds no handle on the picture, reads no recording, and cannot be told anything by one.
!!
!! What crosses. One dict in, told apart by its own `what`: `arming` carries what the drawing is expected to cost and the explanation of why waiting is worth it, as the pane composed them (`{what:'arming', estimate:58, why:'You may need…', started:<ms>}`); `cost` carries how long the drawing took in milliseconds, and the sentence is composed here (`{what:'cost', millis:4587}`); `hide` is the pane going off screen or the run being closed. **Nothing goes back** — there is nothing to report — and **the pane never learns that this module exists**: its messages go to the editor, which relays them, because a module can only `send` to its parent, its sender, or a module it declares itself.
!   -- the module, and the panel it owns --

    script VizAlert
!! @hash 18dee45c
!! @verified 18dee45c
!!!
!! What the module holds from the editor's page, and the state its boundary is kept in.
!!
!! The panel is attached **by id** — `viz.json` renders it, the same way the editor names every element it owns — so the module has a handle on the page without the editor creating anything for it. Both elements are the editor's own as well, which is the arrangement `se-alert` was built for: one panel, one place a message of this kind appears, and the module that owns each writer attaches to the same box by the same id.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **What the panel says, and the facts it is composed from.** `AlertOn` is a drawing that is still going, `AlertUp` whether the panel has been put on screen yet, and `AlertSeconds` how long it has been going — read from the drawing's own clock rather than counted, so this number and the `drew in X.Y s` that replaces it cannot disagree. The *reason* it is worth waiting is not here: the pane composes it with the prediction and sends it, because the pane is where the estimate comes from and an empty reason is the honest answer when there is no rate to judge by.
!   -- the page, by id --
    div AlertPanel
    div AlertText
!   -- the boundary's own state --
    variable AlertIn         ! the dict the editor relayed, as it arrived
    variable AlertWhat       ! which of the three it is
    variable AlertEstimate   ! what the drawing is expected to cost, from the pane
    variable AlertWhy        ! the advice the pane composed for it, if any
    variable AlertStarted    ! the moment the drawing began, on the pane's own clock
    variable AlertMillis     ! how long the drawing took, as the pane measured it
    variable AlertSentence  ! and the sentence made of it, which is this module's own
!   -- the panel's own life --
    variable AlertOn         ! 1 while a drawing is in flight ! §1
    variable AlertUp         ! 1 once the panel is actually on screen
    variable AlertTicking    ! 1 while the ticker runs, because only one of them ever lives
    variable AlertSeconds    ! the drawing's own clock, as the ticker reads it
    variable AlertWaited     ! scratch: milliseconds since the drawing began
    variable AlertWords      ! scratch: the sentence, assembled before it is put up

    attach AlertPanel to `se-alert`
    attach AlertText to `se-alert-text`
    on message go to AlertMessage
    release parent
    stop
!! @hash c57001d5
!! @verified af0ae1b7
!!!
!! The panel's life: armed by a drawing, up when the wait turns out to be long, and away when it is done.
!!
!! Four things decide what it does — whether a drawing is in flight, whether the panel is up, how long the drawing has been going, and what the drawing cost — and **the wait is what decides whether the panel appears at all**: the ticker sleeps a second and only a drawing still running when it wakes has anything to say, so a fast gesture never flashes a box and no threshold is invented. The sentences are composed from what it was told at arming and what it is told at the end, which is why nothing in this module reads the picture.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The panel appears when the wait turns out to be long, and that is decided by the wait rather than by a number chosen in advance.** No threshold is invented: the ticker waits a second, and if the drawing is still going when it wakes, the panel goes up. A drawing that finishes inside that second never shows anything, which is what keeps a fast gesture from flashing a box — and a *first* drawing, on which the pane has no learned rate and so nothing measured to say, is told about exactly as any other. That matters because the first display of a large recording is the case Graham named, and it is the one the pane could otherwise say least about.
!!
!! §2 **Every drawing is covered, and that took a second pass.** The panel used to be armed from the pane's notice of a drawing alone, which only a run's first draw reaches — so a clip, which is the one gesture that reads the whole recording, was silent. Both ends of a drawing now tell it: the pane arms it where every drawing begins and says what it cost where every drawing ends.
!!
!! §3 **Armed for every drawing, and only one ticker ever lives.** The guard is not decoration: a wheel scroll asks for a drawing a frame, and a `fork` per drawing would leave a second of timers stacking up, each of them writing the same panel. One ticker adopts whatever drawing is current, which is all that is wanted — and the clock it reads is that drawing's own, because the arming carries the moment the drawing began.
!!
!! §4 **The count is the drawing's own clock, not the ticker's.** It is measured from the moment the pane hands over at arming, so `N s so far` and the `drew in X.Y s` that replaces it are the same clock read twice — which they were not when this counted from the notice: the ticker's own wait cannot fire while the drawing holds the thread, so its origin would be a moment the reader never sees, and the two numbers would disagree by however long the ticker had been waiting its turn.
!!
!! §5 **The one place the panel's sentence is composed, so its parts cannot disagree.** What the pane is doing, what it expects it to cost (said only when it has a measurement to say it from), how long it has been going, and why it is worth waiting at all. The explanation is the prediction's own text and is empty when the pane has no rate to judge by, so a first drawing reports the count and says nothing about how long it will take — the honest answer rather than a guess.
!!
!! §6 **The end of a drawing, said for every drawing.** The pane says it where every drawing ends, so a clip's finish reaches the panel as a gesture's finish — the fault Graham found, where the first clip of a large recording gave no sign of life at all.
!!
!! §7 **A cost sentence is only said to a panel a reader actually saw.** A drawing that finished inside the ticker's first second never showed anything, and a cost sentence for a message nobody saw would be a box appearing *after* the wait it was meant to explain — which is why a drawing too quick to mention takes the panel away instead.
!!
!! §8 **What the drawing cost, in this module's own words, kept up for a moment and then taken away.** The number arrives as milliseconds and the sentence is assembled here — whole seconds and a tenth of one, because the language's arithmetic is integer-first and a fraction divided straight out would be nought. The pane composes `drew in X.Y` for its own corner line, so this is the same number said twice rather than a second reading of the clock. And the reader's attention is back on the picture: a panel holding a number they have already read is in the way of it, but the number is the one they were waiting for, so it is worth a few seconds.
!!
!! §9 **`AlertOn` is deliberately *not* cleared while the cost is on screen, and that is the one subtlety here.** A drawing that starts while the cost is showing arms the panel for itself, and clearing the flag on the way in would disarm that drawing — so the panel would go missing for the gesture the reader made *because* they had just waited. The flag belongs to the drawing that set it; all the finish does is take the message away if nothing has taken the panel over.
AlertMessage:
    put the message into AlertIn
    put property `what` of AlertIn into AlertWhat
    if AlertWhat is `arming` go to AlertArm
    if AlertWhat is `cost` go to AlertCost
    gosub to AlertHide
    stop

!   ---- armed by a drawing, and told what it expects to cost ----
AlertArm: ! §3
    put property `estimate` of AlertIn into AlertEstimate
    put property `why` of AlertIn into AlertWhy
    put property `started` of AlertIn into AlertStarted
    put 1 into AlertOn
    put 0 into AlertUp
    put 0 into AlertSeconds
    if AlertTicking is 1 stop
    put 1 into AlertTicking
    fork to AlertTick
    stop

AlertTick:
    wait 1 seconds
    ! The ticker is the only thing holding the panel open, so `AlertOn` is how it stops: a drawing that
    ! has finished clears it, and the next tick returns without writing anything.
    if AlertOn is 0
    begin
        ! The drawing is over and the cost has already been said — all the ticker has left to do is stand
        ! itself down, so the next drawing can start one.
        put 0 into AlertTicking
        stop
    end
    if AlertUp is 0 gosub to AlertShow
    put 0 into AlertSeconds ! §4
    if AlertStarted is greater than 0
    begin
        take AlertStarted from now giving AlertWaited
        divide AlertWaited by 1000 giving AlertSeconds
    end
    gosub to AlertSay
    goto AlertTick

AlertShow: ! §1
    put 1 into AlertUp
    set style `display` of AlertPanel to `flex`
    return

AlertSay: ! §5
    put `Working` into AlertWords
    if AlertEstimate is greater than 0 put AlertWords cat ` - about ` cat AlertEstimate cat ` seconds` into AlertWords
    if AlertSeconds is greater than 0 put AlertWords cat ` - ` cat AlertSeconds cat ` s so far` into AlertWords
    if AlertWhy is not empty put AlertWords cat `. ` cat AlertWhy into AlertWords
    set the content of AlertText to AlertWords
    return

!   ---- and told what it cost, when the drawing ends ----
AlertCost: ! §6
    put property `millis` of AlertIn into AlertMillis
    put 0 into AlertOn
    if AlertUp is 0 stop
    if AlertMillis is less than 1
    begin
        gosub to AlertHide ! §7
        stop
    end
    fork to AlertFinish
    stop

AlertFinish: ! §8–§9
    divide AlertMillis by 1000 giving AlertSeconds
    multiply AlertSeconds by 1000 giving AlertWaited
    take AlertWaited from AlertMillis giving AlertWaited
    divide AlertWaited by 100 giving AlertWaited
    put `drew in ` cat AlertSeconds cat `.` cat AlertWaited into AlertSentence
    if AlertUp is 1 set the content of AlertText to AlertSentence cat ` s.`
    wait 4 seconds
    if AlertOn is 1 return
    gosub to AlertHide
    stop
!! @hash 3d2160e9
!! @verified 3d2160e9
!!!
!! The panel out of the way, and the ticker told to stop.
!!
!! Both, always, because either alone leaves one of them running: a hidden panel still being written to once a second, or a ticker stopped with the box still standing. It is reached two ways — the run being closed, and a drawing that ended too quickly to have said anything — so it is a subroutine with its own stop, and the boundary above decides which of those it is.
AlertHide:
    put 0 into AlertOn
    put 0 into AlertUp
    set style `display` of AlertPanel to `none`
    return
!! @hash 94a1cc73
!! @verified 94a1cc73
!!!
